        "text": "Concludes the discussion of Book I with anticipation of what is to come. The Mañjarī's organization broadly follows the sūtras' own architecture, treating the foundational categories first and then the elaborated analyses. Book II Having enumerated the categories in Book I, Gautama now subjects the four pramāṇas to detailed scrutiny. Book II begins by defending the very possibility of pramāṇa against skeptical regress, then examines perception (its conditions, the whole-and-parts question, perception of universals), inference (its types and validity), comparison (its irreducibility to other pramāṇas), and verbal testimony (especially the authority of the Veda and the eternality-of-words debate with Mīmāṃsā). The book is the heart of Nyāya epistemology and the principal site of engagement with Buddhist (Dignāga, Dharmakīrti) and Mīmāṃsaka critics. The first āhnika opens with an elaborate examination of saṃśaya (doubt), since doubt about the very status of pramāṇa motivates the whole investigation. It then takes up perception — Gautama's terse definition (1.1.4) is now defended against multiple objections: that pramāṇas are impossible (regress argument), that perception is only of parts not wholes, that universals are not perceived, that the senses cannot reach their objects. The chapter culminates in establishing perception as a robust, irreducible source of knowledge."
